  <p>
    Those two periods are the entire reason snagging is timed the way it is. The verbatim
    governing text, with its article numbers and issuing authority, belongs on this page — this
    is the page a reader arrives at wanting the citation, and a summary is not a citation:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="UAE Civil Code articles on decennial and defect liability, quoted verbatim with article numbers and authority" />
  </p>

  <h2>Why does the one-year window get missed?</h2>

  <p>
    Because it runs during the least attentive year of ownership. Handover, fit-out, moving in,
    possibly a tenant. Defects that show up seasonally — a balcony that only ponds in heavy
    rain, an air-conditioning unit that only struggles in August — surface months after
    handover, and by the time they are obvious the window is short.
  </p>

  <p>
    The practical defence is to inspect before accepting rather than after moving in. A
    documented snagging report issued at handover predates your occupation of the unit, which
    is a materially different position to reporting the same defect eight months later.{' '}
    <a href="/snagging-dubai/">Snagging in Dubai</a> covers how that inspection is sequenced,
    and it costs AED 666–999 for a typical apartment.
  </p>

  <h2>What do people get wrong about the liability?</h2>

  <p>
    They believe the report is what obliges the developer. It is not. The obligation comes from
    the contract and the law; the report is evidence that a defect existed and when. This
    matters because owners sometimes skip the inspection on the reasoning that they can simply
    report defects as they find them — which is true, and much weaker, because the argument
    then turns on whether the defect was there at handover or arose from use.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second error is accepting a partial fix. The developer closes fifteen of twenty items,
    the owner signs off the list, and the remaining five are now disputed history. Re-inspect
    against the original list, item by item, before signing anything.
  </p>

  <h2>What should you keep, and for how long?</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>The original snagging report, dated, with photographs</li>
    <li>Every item of correspondence with the developer, with dates</li>
    <li>The re-inspection report confirming what was actually closed</li>
    <li>The handover acceptance document and the date on it</li>
    <li>Warranties for installed equipment, which run on their own clocks</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Keep the structural set for ten years, not one. The ten-year period is the one owners
    discard paperwork against, and it is the one where the remedial cost is large enough to
    matter.{' '}
    <a href="/handover-dubai/">The Dubai handover checklist</a> lists what you should be holding
    when you take the keys.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
